      "subtopic": "Meaning of dependency",
      "basis": "panel",
      "ab3": [],
      "stem": "In the context of OOP associations, a dependency is defined as a need for one class to depend on another without a direct association in the same direction. Why are cases with such an association excluded?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "Because Java does not allow an association and a dependency between the same classes",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "Because an association from `Foo` to `Bar` already makes it obvious that `Foo` depends on `Bar`",
          "correct": true
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Because an association makes `Bar` depend on `Foo` instead",
          "correct": false
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "Because dependencies can exist only between interfaces",
          "correct": false
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "If there is an association navigable from `Foo` to `Bar`, `Foo` obviously depends on `Bar`, so there is no point mentioning the dependency separately; the concept focuses on non-obvious dependencies.",
      "optional": false
